00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:01.229
All right.

00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:04.940
A very, very big week for the David Pakman
show, the Joe Rogan appearance.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.750
A lot of other stuff.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.999
Um, so let's go to the phones and see what's
on people's minds at six one seven, eight,

00:00:10.999 --> 00:00:13.110
three zero 47 50.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.800
Um, let's start with our color from the two
oh five area code.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.690
Who's calling today from two oh five.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:20.900
Hey, good.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.520
This is a, this is Corey.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.410
Welcome back from California.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.410
Thanks Corey.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:28.999
What's going on?

00:00:28.999 --> 00:00:36.880
So when I watched your interview, uh, I think
it was for the second time with are talking

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:43.800
about college and he pushed back about, uh,
the trades and emphasizing the trades more

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:50.829
and not, and not being for, you know, in particular,
fully subsidizing, let's say a university

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:51.829
based education.

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:56.960
One of the things that came to mind that I
was wanting to ask you for a while is why

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:05.870
not, uh, instead push for fully funding trades
maybe as a starting point, uh, and then to

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:13.110
expand, let's say towards, uh, subsidize or,
you know, fully subsidized university education.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.110
Well, what would it be?

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.730
The reason to start with trades?

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:19.760
I mean, so you could start anywhere or you
could do both or you can do neither.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.750
But what would be the reason to do trades?

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:22.750
First?

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:28.380
How would that help us eventually get to subsidizing
fully subsidizing, uh, State College?

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:35.320
Well, I think the thought would be, at least
in my mind, David would be that here you are

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.440
tackling what would be more in demand right
now.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:46.460
So taking, taking Andrews, uh, concern, let's
say that that trades are heavily under represented

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:52.450
in the United Sates versus many of the other,
uh, European countries in particular focusing

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:58.900
on trades first as a way to, you know, not
just get a better representation but also,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:05.430
uh, start to mitigate some of the incoming
problems around automation and how that might

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:09.849
affect, um, job opportunities in the United
States.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:16.579
Listen, I don't know that the automation question
is going to be better handled by more trade

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:19.280
school versus more bachelor's degrees.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.549
I think that really the fundamental question
around automation is going to be what portion

00:02:24.549 --> 00:02:30.730
of the gains from automation go to the business
owners versus the employees.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:35.329
And the way to think about it would be imagine
a company with a 100 employees.

00:02:35.329 --> 00:02:44.390
Um, automation makes it so that, um, do you,
you only need 50 employees plus some machines

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.249
to get the same level of productivity.

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:50.549
There's basically like three possibilities
for how those gains were used in sort of like

00:02:50.549 --> 00:02:53.489
a purely vulture capitalist system.

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:58.700
You would just lay off the 50 employees, you
would keep only 50, and the company gets all

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.959
of the gains of that technology.

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:08.510
In a socialist system, you would keep all
100 employees and the doubling of productivity

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:14.019
from the technology would allow every single
employee to just work half as much in like

00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:19.670
a social democracy setting, we would say,
okay, some portion of the gains from that

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:26.120
technology can go to the business owner and
then some portion should be distributed among

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.120
the employees.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.730
And where you set that line, like is it 5%
or 95% for the employees?

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.719
Depends sort of on your political philosophy.

00:03:33.719 --> 00:03:35.730
I don't, that's a reality regardless.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:41.700
I mean, that's gonna happen in both, uh, program,
uh, school, uh, jobs rather, that you need

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.780
a bachelor's degree for and jobs that you
would go to trade school for.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.629
And I think it's just a broader economic question.

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:50.630
I don't know that we solve it more or less
by saying we're going to focus on funding

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.499
trade over bachelor's degrees.

00:03:52.499 --> 00:03:53.680
Okay.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.590
Thanks for the response.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.129
All right, great to hear it from you, Corey.

00:03:57.129 --> 00:03:58.239
I appreciate it very much.

